{{also|rita|Ríta|ritā|rīta|rītā}}
==English==

===Etymology===
A shortening of {{der|en|it|Margherita}}, and {{der|en|es|Margarita}}, cognates of English [[Margaret]]. An Italian [[w:Rita of Cascia|Saint Rita]] was [[canonize]]d in 1900. The English name came into regular use in early twentieth century.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|en|iːtə}}

===Proper noun===
{{en-proper noun}}

# {{given name|en|female|from=Italian}}.
#* '''1993''' Diane Telgen, Jim Kamp (editors), ''Notable Hispanic American Women'', VNR AG, {{ISBN|0810375788}}, page 194:
#*: Born Margarita Carmen Cansino to Eduardo and Volga Haworth<!-- sic--> Cansino on October 17, 1918, in New York City, '''Rita''' Hayworth was no stranger to show business.

